Subject: Results in mirt and in IRTPRO 3 program
Message-ID: <qCI-DdGMppU.YDrLeLMjKAAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/qCI-DdGMppU/m/YDrLeLMjKAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Can someone explain why I obtain different a, b, and c parameters estimates when I use IRTPRO3 and the package mirt from R?

The method both uses are Bock and Aiken (1981) - IRTPRO calls BAEM and R calls EM.

I have estimated the parameters with default options both in IRTPRO and with mirt package, but the results are quite different.

My sample consists of 96 items with 180 respondents.

In IRTPRO the configurations (from mysets.irtpro) are

    Estimation:
        Method = BAEM;
        E-Step = 500, 1e-005;
        SE = S-EM;
        M-Step = 50, 1e-006;
        Quadrature = 49, 6;
        SEM = 0.001;
        SS = 1e-005;
    Scoring:
        Mean = 0;
        SD = 1;
    Priors:
        (Q_1, Slope[0]) = Lognormal, 0, 1;
        (Q_1, Guessing[0]) = Beta, 4, 16;
        (Q_2, Slope[0]) = Lognormal, 0, 1;
        (Q_2, Guessing[0]) = Beta, 4, 16;
        (Q_3, Slope[0]) = Lognormal, 0, 1;
        (Q_3, Guessing[0]) = Beta, 4, 16;
        (Q_4, Slope[0]) = Lognormal, 0, 1;
        (Q_4, Guessing[0]) = Beta, 4, 16;
        (Q_5, Slope[0]) = Lognormal, 0, 1;
        (Q_5, Guessing[0]) = Beta, 4, 16;
    .
    .
    .

And in R I ran

    mirt(test_result, 1, itemtype='3PL')

The first 10 results for estimated parameter in **R** are

               a        b     g u
    Q_1    0.413   -3.454 0.062 1
    Q_2    0.807   -0.115 0.262 1
    Q_3   -0.460    3.967 0.249 1
    Q_4    3.827   -1.121 0.962 1
    Q_5    2.896    0.090 0.636 1
    Q_6    0.328   -1.556 0.030 1
    Q_7    0.931    2.879 0.003 1
    Q_8   -5.402   -2.415 0.058 1
    Q_9    2.810   -0.406 0.810 1
    Q_10   0.003  -18.079 0.161 1

The first 10 results in **IRTPRO** are

               a                       b                     g
    Q_1        0.39                    -2.92                    0.2
    Q_2        0.77                    -0.12                    0.19
    Q_3        0.13                    -14.18                    0.2
    Q_4        0.8                        -5.77                    0.2
    Q_5        0.69                    -1.7                    0.2
    Q_6        0.31                    -0.36                    0.19
    Q_7        0.37                    109220.43                0.09
    Q_8        0.37                    26016.14                0.08
    Q_9        0.95                    -2.75                    0.2
    Q_10    0.12                    0.43                    0.21

I see that there are priors in IRTPRO estimates ( (Q_1, Slope[0]) = Lognormal, 0, 1; (Q_1, Guessing[0]) = Beta, 4, 16; ). Is that the case to set this priors in mirt? If so, what is that configurations. I tried to use parprior option, but without success.

I'm very new to IRT analysis. But I need to get the same results in IRTPRO and in mirt to validate de estimates in IRTPRO.

Can someone help me?

From ub02cc32c527f7c33 at groups.google.com  Wed Sep  2 07:37:22 2015
From: ub02cc32c527f7c33 at groups.google.com (Scott Withrow)
Date: Wed, 02 Sep 2015 07:37:22 +0000
Subject: Parameters in MIRT versus Simulated Parameters
Message-ID: <hlTqcvf1slg.nWfN_flSKAAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/hlTqcvf1slg/m/nWfN_flSKAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

A little story time. A few days ago I was building an IRT demonstration using MIRT and I wanted to demonstrate how some specific item parameters function so I generated item parameters and responses using Wingen3. When I estimated the item parameters from the simulated responses I noticed there were a couple of oddly high a1 parameter estimates for a couple of items. I thought this was a little strange so I did a quick little simulation looking at a number of 50 item tests taken by 500, 1000, 2000, and 5000 respondents (100 replications per cell) with dichotomous responses scored through the 3PL to see how well MIRT was recovering parameters. I was a little surprised at what resulted.

>From a correlation standpoint MIRT performs quite well with recovery in the .8-.98 range. However, more surprising to me was when I looked at the deviation between estimated and simulated parameters there is a huge difference in a parameters. On average in each 50 item set a number of items have an a parameter 1 full point higher and a few items with an a parameter 2 points higher. It works out to be 99% of tests are affected by this with 500 responses or less and 74% of tests with 1000 responses. Even with 2000 responses 38% of tests had at least 1 a parameter having a difference of 1 and 12% having a difference of 2. The d and g parameters do not usually exhibit deviations more than 1 point higher.

I put together a table to help explain

                        Parameter Deviations

                        More than 1                              More than 2

                        a          d          g          a          d          g

500       Min       0          0          0          0          0          0

            Max      11        3          0          2          5          0

            Mean    4.05      0.46      0          0.12      1.06      0

            Median 4          0          0          0          1          0

            Mode   4          0          0          0          0          0

            % tests affected           99        39        0          11        64        0

                        Simulated vs Recovered Mean Correlations

                        0.823    0.979    0.683

1000     Min       0          0          0          0          0          0

            Max      5          2          1          1          0          0

            Mean    1.41      0.37      0.12      0.04      0          0

            Median 1          0          0          0          0          0

            Mode   1          0          0          0          0          0

            % tests affected           74        30        12        4          0          0

                        Simulated vs Recovered Mean Correlations

                        0.922    0.990    0.794

2000     Min       0          0          0          0          0          0

            Max      3          2          2          2          0          0

            Mean    0.47      0.22      0.24      0.13      0          0

            Median 0          0          0          0          0          0

            Mode   0          0          0          0          0          0

            % tests affected           38        20        23        12        0          0

                        Simulated vs Recovered Mean Correlations

                        0.954    0.992    0.858

5000     Min       0          0          0          0          0          0

            Max      1          1          1          1          0          0

            Mean    0.11      0.03      0.03      0.01      0          0

            Median 0          0          0          0          0          0

            Mode   0          0          0          0          0          0

            % tests affected           11        3          3          1          0          0

                        Simulated vs Recovered Mean Correlations

                        0.985    0.998    0.930

Here is a link to the table in an excel format: https://dl.dropboxusercontent.com/u/510411/MIRTSimResults.xlsx

What is causing this odd behavior? I spot checked the responses being generated with WinGen3 in MULTILOG and got very similar responses to the simulated results so I don’t believe it’s an issue with how WinGen3 is simulation responses.

Best,

Scott

